-- This recipe finds a Roc value read after a write took it, within one
-- function: the shape behind every copy roc-apps' copy shapes could make on
-- Roc 0.1.0-RC1 (its tools/copy_shapes/, and docs/roc-notes.md, "Speed and
-- copying"). Roc writes into a list, a string or a dict in place only when
-- nothing else refers to it, and nothing says when it copies instead: a
-- `List.set` on a list that something reads afterwards copies the whole
-- list, on every write. It reads each file's tree from Roc's own parser,
-- and walks each function and each lambda in evaluation order, tracking
-- each value by path (`xs`, `st.items`), branch by branch and through a
-- loop twice. A finding says "may copy": the compiler inlines and
-- specializes before it decides, so the release build sometimes writes in
-- place where the rule (which models the dev build) can't see it. There are
-- five checks:
--
--   after_consume        A path read after a consuming call took it, or
--                        taken twice: `ys = xs.set(j, v) ?? xs` then
--                        `xs.get(j)`, a loop that takes the same list on
--                        every pass, a `trim` then the old string's `concat`.
--   whole_record         A record read whole after a consuming call took one
--                        of its fields, or inside that call's own arguments:
--                        `{ ..d, cells: d.cells.set(kept(d, j), v) ?? ... }`,
--                        which copies in the dev and boxy builds. Bind what
--                        `kept` gives on the line before.
--   closure              A closure called after a write took a value it
--                        holds (`at = |k| xs.get(k) ?? ...`, the write, then
--                        `at(j)`), or a closure that writes a value its maker
--                        still holds.
--   held                 A value got from a container (`get`, `first`,
--                        `last`, or a `match` on one) and written while the
--                        container still holds it and is read again (or is
--                        the call the write is an argument of): an inner list
--                        got and set back, a dict's value got and inserted
--                        back. Take it out first (`remove`, `replace`), or
--                        write it with `update`.
--   restored_refcounted  With `types=1`, `?? xs` handing back a list whose
--                        entries hold a reference count (`List(Str)`), which
--                        copies on every write in the dev and boxy builds.
--
-- **What consumes** is a table in Consume.roc: RC1's uniqueness-checked
-- List and Str builtins (set, replace, swap, concat, prepend, reserve,
-- drop_at, reverse, append; Str's concat, trims, case maps and reserve, but
-- not sublist and the drops and takes, which slice a shared list without a
-- copy), List's update, map and sorts, Dict's and
-- Set's insert, remove and update, and roc-apps' Unique marker's unwrap and
-- take_from. A module call (`List.set(xs, ...)`) consumes its first
-- argument, a method call (`xs.set(...)`) its receiver, whatever the
-- receiver's type. A call of the program's own function consumes nothing:
-- the rule is within one function. **`?? xs` is no use**: the fallback of a
-- consuming call that names its own target hands it back, and RC1 sees
-- through that. **A read before the write is free**, inside the write's own
-- arguments too (`xs.set(j, at(j) + 1)`), except a record's whole read
-- inside the write of one of its fields.
--
-- **What it over-reports**, by design: a list's `len` read after its write
-- within one function, which RC1 writes in place though a `get` there copies
-- (the plants' over_reports.roc); and `map` or a sort of a list read after,
-- where a new list is made either way.
--
-- The recipe prints a summary line of counts, one line a finding
-- (`path:line: check: why`), and the summary again. A file that does not
-- parse is listed after the findings and fails the run. Findings are a
-- report: a caller holds their count.
--
--   cd <repo> && shale <shale>/cookbook/roc/find_uses_after_a_consume.shale glob='basic/roc/*.roc'
--   cd <repo> && shale <shale>/cookbook/roc/find_uses_after_a_consume.shale glob='app/*.roc' types=1 root=app/main.roc
--   shale cookbook:roc/find_uses_after_a_consume.shale self_test=1
--
-- `self_test=1` runs the checks over the plants in
-- cookbook/roc/plants/find_uses_after_a_consume/ (and its typed/ folder,
-- with the types and the root typed/StringLists.roc), each a pair of
-- roc-apps' copy shapes: a line marked `# <check>` must give that finding
-- and no unmarked line one, so the member of a pair that writes in place
-- stays clean and the one that copies is found.
--
-- It is written in Roc, the app
-- cookbook/roc/find_uses_after_a_consume/find_uses_after_a_consume.roc, the
-- modules beside it (Consume, the rule; Types, roc-types run; SelfTest) and
-- the cookbook's Roc library (cookbook/roc/lib/: Forest, Finding and
-- RocTypes), on Shale's Roc platform (roc_platform/README.md); this file is
-- its launcher, so it is launched as every Shale recipe is.
--
-- **The launcher** hands every value it is given to roc_platform/run.shale in
-- the checkout this cookbook is in (the `cookbook` mount's parent), which
-- builds the Roc there (cached; the first build after a change to C Shale
-- compiles the platform's host, 25 to 50 s) and runs the program rooted
-- here, so `glob=` and `root=` are this root's paths. A value the Roc script
-- reads no `Shale.arg!` of stops the run before anything is built. Its
-- output is the program's, then the launcher's own stop when it stops.
-- `reads=inputs` builds and runs nothing, and prints the real path of each
-- file the program is built from, one a line (run.shale's header), for a
-- caller's cache key. It is Roc's dev build.
-- budget: programs=10 output_bytes=8000000

const SCRIPT = "cookbook/roc/find_uses_after_a_consume/find_uses_after_a_consume.roc"
const CHECKOUT = dirname(dirname(real("cookbook:README.md")))
const HERE = real(".")

-- `self_test` is read here by name, as the cookbook's full tier finds the
-- recipes it runs `self_test=1` of. `arg_names()` is every value given; the
-- rest are read by a name made as the run goes, so Shale's own check cannot
-- see them: run.shale holds them to the script's reads.
const SELF_TEST = arg("self_test", "")
local values = {"script=" .. SCRIPT, "in=" .. HERE}
if SELF_TEST ~= "" then push(values, "self_test=" .. SELF_TEST) end
for name in arg_names() do
  if name ~= "self_test" then push(values, name .. "=" .. arg(name, "")) end
end
local r = run({"shale", "--root", CHECKOUT, CHECKOUT .. "/roc_platform/run.shale", "--args-stdin"}, {stdin = join(values, "\n") .. "\n", timeout_ms = 1800000, whole = 8000000})
print(trim_end(r.out))
if r.code ~= 0 then fail("find_uses_after_a_consume: the Roc program stopped (exit " .. r.code .. ")") end
